WEBVTT

00:02.335 --> 00:06.130
It looks like we've got to grab
those rubber trees

00:06.131 --> 00:08.842
while racing against this
Stanley Snyder guy at full speed!

00:12.762 --> 00:15.514
This sounds like fun! Leave it to me!

00:15.515 --> 00:18.351
I know a few places
where we could find some!

00:20.687 --> 00:22.856
We'll use them to craft some motorbikes

00:23.648 --> 00:26.192
and blow past South America!

00:27.652 --> 00:29.112
ORIGINAL WORK: RIICHIRO INAGAKI, BOICHI
SHUEISHA "JUMP COMICS"

02:03.081 --> 02:04.123
Right now, we're here.

02:04.124 --> 02:05.374
I'll show you a place

02:05.375 --> 02:07.960
near the sea in Panama
where you can get rubber.

02:07.961 --> 02:11.380
But if we take our time
collecting that rubber...

02:11.381 --> 02:14.758
Stanley will probably catch up...

02:14.759 --> 02:17.553
It's not like I'm scared
or anything, though...

02:17.554 --> 02:21.098
Well, yes...
That could actually be a problem.

02:21.099 --> 02:22.558
Jeez!

02:22.559 --> 02:24.978
You really have no idea
how terrible Stanley actually is.

02:25.562 --> 02:28.606
If only you had seen the incident
when he sniped Senku down...

02:29.232 --> 02:35.195
<i>If Stanley catches this boat
with his plane from the aircraft carrier,</i>

02:35.196 --> 02:36.406
<i>it's all over!</i>

02:38.199 --> 02:39.826
I guess you're right.

02:41.661 --> 02:44.371
And now that they know
the recipe for the Revival Fluid,

02:44.372 --> 02:46.748
there's no reason
for them to spare our lives.

02:46.749 --> 02:48.292
Please, Ukyo!

02:48.293 --> 02:51.753
Can't we somehow lose Stanley's ship

02:51.754 --> 02:55.716
<i>with some kind of radar battle?</i>

02:55.717 --> 02:58.010
No, if I emit any radar waves,

02:58.011 --> 03:00.013
I'll just give away our position.

03:01.014 --> 03:02.723
<i>From what I can hear,</i>

03:02.724 --> 03:05.268
<i>the enemy's radar guy is a total elite.</i>

03:06.102 --> 03:08.437
<i>They won't lose sight of their enemy.</i>

03:08.438 --> 03:11.065
<i>They're sticking right to us,
even at half our speed.</i>

03:12.525 --> 03:14.819
<i>We can't lose them.</i>

03:15.445 --> 03:18.196
If only we could use the Panama Canal...

03:18.197 --> 03:19.698
"Banana Camel"?

03:19.699 --> 03:21.868
With all due respect, let me explain.

03:22.368 --> 03:23.994
FRANCOIS'S GEOGRAPHY CLASS

03:23.995 --> 03:26.456
Welcome to Francois's Geography Class.

03:28.082 --> 03:32.044
If you were to travel by ship
to the sea on the other side,

03:32.045 --> 03:34.713
don't you think
this would be quite a hassle?

03:34.714 --> 03:36.174
<i>That would be a huge pain.</i>

03:36.799 --> 03:38.968
That is why,
at the beginning of the 20th century,

03:39.886 --> 03:42.680
<i>humanity cut through
this narrow strip of land</i>

03:43.181 --> 03:45.891
<i>and connected the two oceans
with a waterway.</i>

03:45.892 --> 03:48.144
This is the Panama Canal.

03:48.770 --> 03:50.772
This concludes my explanation.

03:52.398 --> 03:54.108
FRANCOIS'S GEOGRAPHY CLASS
THE END

03:54.734 --> 03:55.901
Is this real?

03:55.902 --> 03:58.695
The scientific world
made the impossible happen!

03:58.696 --> 04:02.241
If we cross it and reach the ocean
on the other side, we win.

04:02.242 --> 04:04.201
The canal is so damaged and dented

04:04.202 --> 04:07.247
that there's no way Stanley's
giant aircraft carrier could get through.

04:08.623 --> 04:12.334
Let's head to that Banana Cam... nal then!

04:12.335 --> 04:15.128
I don't think so.
It's been abandoned for millennia.

04:15.129 --> 04:17.756
It's probably buried
deep underground by now.

04:17.757 --> 04:19.050
That's it!

04:20.927 --> 04:22.261
We'll split into two groups:

04:22.262 --> 04:25.639
one will go after the rubber trees,
the other after the Panama Canal.

04:25.640 --> 04:26.640
Huh?

04:26.641 --> 04:28.141
While we're out at sea?

04:28.142 --> 04:29.143
But how?

04:30.353 --> 04:32.938
The ones heading to the Panama Canal
are just a decoy, right?

04:32.939 --> 04:34.064
Yes.

04:34.065 --> 04:35.900
Let's attach a bunch of flashy decoys

04:36.567 --> 04:39.570
to make the radar cross-section
look like the real deal.

04:40.071 --> 04:42.489
Their radar guy is way too good.

04:42.490 --> 04:45.076
He won't overlook it even if it's a decoy.

04:45.576 --> 04:46.994
That's what we'll use against him.

04:46.995 --> 04:50.747
Still, the Panama Canal is blocked, right?

04:50.748 --> 04:54.335
We can't be ten billion percent sure,
but it probably is.

04:54.919 --> 04:58.131
And then we'll hit a dead end,
and Stanley will finish us off.

04:58.631 --> 04:59.799
Yes.

05:00.591 --> 05:02.843
Our decoy group at the Panama Canal

05:02.844 --> 05:04.928
will be shot and killed.

05:04.929 --> 05:07.931
That strategy is a no-go.

05:07.932 --> 05:11.059
We have no choice, that's our best option.

05:11.060 --> 05:13.520
Are you serious, Ukyo?

05:13.521 --> 05:16.356
For someone who clearly
hates that kind of stuff

05:16.357 --> 05:18.985
to say something like that...

05:20.862 --> 05:24.699
We'll part ways here,
for the rest of eternity.

05:25.616 --> 05:26.701
But who...?

05:32.248 --> 05:35.959
The Lab Car?

05:35.960 --> 05:38.337
DRIVERLESS DRIVING

05:40.715 --> 05:43.133
My poor, poor baby...

05:43.134 --> 05:46.595
Senku! You helped make it too, didn't you?

05:46.596 --> 05:48.472
Don't you feel sorry for it?

05:48.473 --> 05:50.974
No, it's just a machine.

05:50.975 --> 05:52.684
I took the lab instruments from inside.

05:52.685 --> 05:56.439
No, that's so cold of you!

05:58.232 --> 05:59.066
"THE LAB CAR WAS A FRIEND" SIDE

05:59.067 --> 06:00.609
"A MACHINE IS JUST THAT" SIDE

06:00.610 --> 06:04.322
So the Japanese team isn't
exactly united on that either, huh?

06:08.201 --> 06:11.287
There are now two ships
from the Kids' Science Club.

06:11.788 --> 06:14.248
One of them is heading
to the Panama Canal.

06:14.749 --> 06:17.793
Wait, the Panama Canal still exists?

06:17.794 --> 06:19.795
Nobody knows it.

06:19.796 --> 06:23.132
But if it does, they'd get through,
and we'd be completely trapped.

06:26.511 --> 06:28.929
Because of the old humanity's wisdom,
the Panama Canal,

06:28.930 --> 06:30.931
even if they know it's a decoy,

06:30.932 --> 06:33.475
they've got no choice
but to chase us at full speed.

06:33.476 --> 06:36.604
This is how things are, Stanley...

06:38.022 --> 06:42.193
So, you've come up with
a few clever tricks, huh?

07:10.430 --> 07:11.722
Here they are!

07:14.142 --> 07:16.101
These look like simple trees!

07:16.102 --> 07:18.479
Is that rare rubber really here?

07:21.274 --> 07:23.693
We just need to make a cut
in the rubber tree

07:24.193 --> 07:26.736
and collect as much sap as we can.

07:26.737 --> 07:28.405
Wait a sec!

07:28.406 --> 07:31.117
Isn't it the same as
collecting lacquer sap?

07:32.577 --> 07:35.287
This seems like a job
for an Ishigami Villager.

07:35.288 --> 07:38.416
I've done this before too!

07:53.973 --> 07:54.807
Are you sure?

07:55.558 --> 07:58.060
You're giving a weapon to a hostage.

07:58.686 --> 08:01.438
Xeno, you're a super scientist, right?

08:01.439 --> 08:04.524
If you get the chance,
you'll help us make the rubber.

08:04.525 --> 08:09.154
If our rides were to break down,
we would fall and die.

08:09.155 --> 08:11.949
Besides, Xeno, you're just like Senku.

08:12.867 --> 08:15.203
You can't lie when it comes to science.

08:16.204 --> 08:17.288
Right?

08:21.501 --> 08:25.212
I'll just be sure to keep you away
from any communicators.

08:25.213 --> 08:26.672
Oh, what a pity.

08:27.173 --> 08:30.259
Looks like you're not just a big,
naive fool after all.

08:41.604 --> 08:44.022
This is the same sap from before?

08:44.023 --> 08:46.234
Now it looks jiggly, like Bavarian cream.

08:46.817 --> 08:47.984
Solidify it with vinegar,

08:47.985 --> 08:51.697
mix in sulfur powder,
and knead it thoroughly with rollers...

08:57.828 --> 09:00.080
It's super bouncy!

09:00.081 --> 09:02.500
YOU FOUND RUBBER

09:06.546 --> 09:08.463
What is this?

09:08.464 --> 09:12.468
We can pull and stretch it like this!
It's a wonder!

09:14.136 --> 09:15.428
Someone from the 21st century

09:15.429 --> 09:18.849
would see a tragedy coming if you do this!

09:22.228 --> 09:24.021
As expected...

09:25.398 --> 09:27.524
Chrome, I'm sorry...

09:27.525 --> 09:29.026
This rubber is awesome!

09:29.652 --> 09:31.278
It's so interesting!

09:31.279 --> 09:34.740
The liquid from an ordinary tree
can stretch and turn into a spring!

09:35.658 --> 09:38.411
Yes, in fact,
it's an interesting material.

09:39.036 --> 09:41.830
But it's probably too soft
for any kind of crafting.

09:41.831 --> 09:43.873
Besides playing, what's it good for?

09:43.874 --> 09:44.916
- Awesome!
- Awesome!

09:44.917 --> 09:46.751
Yes, you're right.

09:46.752 --> 09:48.878
Without scientific processing,

09:48.879 --> 09:52.382
even the god-tier material rubber
is just an amusing toy.

09:52.383 --> 09:54.718
In fact, historically speaking,

09:54.719 --> 09:57.762
after Columbus introduced it
to modern civilization,

09:57.763 --> 10:01.016
it was abandoned for nearly 200 years.

10:01.017 --> 10:02.934
We need to process it, Taiju.

10:02.935 --> 10:05.396
I'll leave the physical work to you.

10:07.440 --> 10:10.151
It had been a while since
I last heard that!

10:12.361 --> 10:14.112
The rubber turned black!

10:14.113 --> 10:18.533
We'll mix in powdered sulfur and carbon
with the rubber, heat it with the boiler,

10:18.534 --> 10:21.745
then stretch it to its limits with
Taiju's full power until it's all gooey.

10:21.746 --> 10:22.663
Then...

10:24.040 --> 10:25.082
Stamp it!

10:32.423 --> 10:34.925
It's got such beautiful
ridges and grooves!

10:35.843 --> 10:38.012
It's started to look like the real thing!

10:38.888 --> 10:42.183
We'll make the rubber
for the balloon part separately, right?

10:42.892 --> 10:43.934
Of course.

10:45.311 --> 10:46.478
What is it, Xeno?

10:46.479 --> 10:49.565
You're being awfully cooperative
for an enemy, aren't you?

10:50.232 --> 10:52.401
It's for my own safety.

10:52.902 --> 10:54.694
I don't care if you Science Club kids

10:54.695 --> 10:57.697
are going to ruin yourselves
with your amateurish science,

10:57.698 --> 10:59.283
but I won't let that impact me.

11:00.034 --> 11:01.326
Of course,

11:01.327 --> 11:04.997
we don't need to worry
as long as Senku is in charge.

11:09.085 --> 11:11.379
The whole thing ended up all bumpy.

11:12.463 --> 11:15.298
Now, we fit that into a round mold.

11:15.299 --> 11:18.051
And mix in the wire and fabric too.

11:18.052 --> 11:19.803
Then after baking and cooling,

11:19.804 --> 11:22.306
the tire casing is complete.

11:24.141 --> 11:27.227
Then we make a rubber tube
to inflate it from the inside

11:27.228 --> 11:29.522
and secure it
with the internal wires and such.

11:31.774 --> 11:35.194
Now all I have to do is
breathe life into it with my willpower!

11:37.238 --> 11:39.031
No, we'll use an air pump.

11:40.699 --> 11:41.825
YOU'VE GOT TIRES!!

11:41.826 --> 11:43.034
Could these be...?

11:43.035 --> 11:43.953
Yes.

11:44.537 --> 11:46.371
It's the legs
of a new dimension of science,

11:46.372 --> 11:48.957
a breakthrough with which humanity

11:48.958 --> 11:51.710
can conquer fields at lightning speed.

12:00.594 --> 12:02.137
So, this is rubber!

12:02.138 --> 12:06.058
The speed and comfort are on a whole
different level compared to bamboo wheels!

12:07.810 --> 12:10.186
These tires will let us go off-road!

12:10.187 --> 12:12.355
We still need to make
the motorbikes' bodies.

12:12.356 --> 12:14.190
We don't have much time, though.

12:14.191 --> 12:17.069
We really never have much time.

12:19.655 --> 12:21.407
RUMBLE-RUMBLE

12:22.116 --> 12:24.242
Hey! What did you do, old man?

12:24.243 --> 12:26.953
You're wrecking the ship!

12:26.954 --> 12:28.705
I'm just collecting materials.

12:28.706 --> 12:31.708
Yes, working on board is our only option.

12:31.709 --> 12:33.502
We'll craft as we move forward.

12:35.004 --> 12:39.467
Even if the Lab Car decoy bought us time,
we don't how many more days we have.

12:43.929 --> 12:45.306
Enemy ship, four kilometers away.

12:45.890 --> 12:48.017
It entered our visual range.

12:55.399 --> 12:56.942
This kind of movement...

12:58.444 --> 13:00.154
It's a crewless decoy.

13:07.161 --> 13:09.078
No one's coming out.

13:09.079 --> 13:12.583
As we thought,
the real ship was the one heading south.

13:13.209 --> 13:15.920
Turn around! Full speed!

13:19.590 --> 13:21.132
We're here!

13:21.133 --> 13:22.759
At this place in Ecuador.

13:22.760 --> 13:24.970
We'll be landing here.

13:29.850 --> 13:31.768
This looks terrible.

13:31.769 --> 13:34.479
The desert has come all the way here.

13:34.480 --> 13:36.023
The passing of time is terrifying.

13:36.607 --> 13:39.151
It's better this way. We can run faster.

13:40.236 --> 13:42.947
We won't be able to hide
while moving, though.

13:45.950 --> 13:47.033
Ha...?

13:47.034 --> 13:49.119
They wrecked it all!

13:49.870 --> 13:53.122
Yes, we're not riding this ship anymore.

13:53.123 --> 13:57.670
We're really crossing South America,
modifying our vehicles as we go.

13:58.921 --> 14:01.924
Let's gather all the wood too!
It'll become our fuel!

14:02.424 --> 14:03.716
The wood...

14:03.717 --> 14:04.801
Into fuel?

14:04.802 --> 14:07.011
Can motorbikes be fueled with wood?

14:07.012 --> 14:08.597
This sounds awesome!

14:09.223 --> 14:12.809
To be precise, they run on gas
produced by burning wood.

14:12.810 --> 14:17.314
We'll make one big motorbike
and five small ones.

14:18.148 --> 14:20.900
<i>We'll burn the wood in a boiler
inside the big motorbike,</i>

14:20.901 --> 14:23.487
<i>then fill the gas tank
with the resulting charcoal gas.</i>

14:23.988 --> 14:28.367
The small bikes will run by jamming that
fuel tank straight into their engines.

14:28.868 --> 14:30.451
Wait! Wait!

14:30.452 --> 14:33.121
The boiler is just fine
since it's like a furnace.

14:33.122 --> 14:34.539
But six engines?

14:34.540 --> 14:36.833
It'll take ages to make them,

14:36.834 --> 14:40.337
and Stanley is still chasing us.
How are we going to do this?

14:42.464 --> 14:44.925
Six engines powered by charcoal gas...

14:46.010 --> 14:46.968
We've got the boiler!

14:46.969 --> 14:49.680
And the six engines too!

14:51.098 --> 14:54.059
So that's why we're getting
six motorbikes, right?

14:54.894 --> 14:57.855
Everyone! The Lab Car was shot down!

14:59.023 --> 15:01.483
I can tell from Stanley's radar signal.

15:02.234 --> 15:05.112
They've turned around,
and they're heading here at full speed!

15:07.406 --> 15:09.324
Our deadline is tight!

15:09.325 --> 15:11.910
It's going to be days and nights
of hard work!

15:11.911 --> 15:16.248
Let's unleash our desire
to complete the final product!

15:52.451 --> 15:53.702
They're finally here!

15:54.286 --> 15:55.788
It's Stanley's ship!

16:02.711 --> 16:03.629
Huh?

16:07.257 --> 16:10.260
They're going to kill
Senku and the others!

16:10.761 --> 16:13.931
Surround them as soon as we hit shore.
Don't let off a single soul!

16:16.266 --> 16:19.186
Everyone! Run!

16:51.927 --> 16:53.052
Motorbikes?

16:53.053 --> 16:54.805
How did they...?

16:55.848 --> 16:58.600
Did they make them
while traveling on board?

16:59.184 --> 17:02.771
Motorbikes are awesome!
What is this kind of feeling?

17:03.480 --> 17:06.649
Have we broken through Stanley's blockade?

17:06.650 --> 17:07.734
Not yet!

17:07.735 --> 17:11.404
It's just the first layer of the net!
A direct surround and hold-up!

17:11.405 --> 17:12.572
Yes.

17:12.573 --> 17:16.368
The second layer,
long-range concentrated fire is coming!

17:16.952 --> 17:19.955
But, if we can just break through here...

17:20.706 --> 17:24.710
<i>We can finally get to the source
of the petrification beam!</i>

17:25.210 --> 17:27.087
Fire!

17:30.883 --> 17:34.469
Are you sure you don't need
your hostage as a shield?

17:34.470 --> 17:37.305
Yes, I'm sure...

17:37.306 --> 17:38.724
Have you no mercy?

17:39.224 --> 17:43.603
Hyoga's the only one who'd pull something
like this without a second thought...

17:43.604 --> 17:45.730
It's strange, though...

17:45.731 --> 17:48.399
Why would someone as
careful and put-together as Xeno

17:48.400 --> 17:51.612
deliberately expose himself to danger?

17:52.112 --> 17:54.615
It's almost like he wants
to get to the frontline...

17:55.199 --> 17:57.575
Carlos! I'll be blunt!

17:57.576 --> 18:00.411
You were a professional driver, right?

18:00.412 --> 18:02.330
Can you help me make a smoke screen?

18:02.331 --> 18:04.791
Of course! Don't underestimate me!

18:04.792 --> 18:06.043
I'm a pro!

18:17.262 --> 18:20.349
Idiot! Cease fire! You'll hit Xeno!

18:21.809 --> 18:24.352
But we can't see what's
in front of us either!

18:24.353 --> 18:25.728
All right!

18:25.729 --> 18:28.147
The second net is breached too!

18:28.148 --> 18:29.233
Yay!

18:34.279 --> 18:35.363
He's coming!

18:35.364 --> 18:37.949
Form a line with Xeno at the front
as your shield!

18:37.950 --> 18:41.369
He's aiming for our heads!
Everyone, duck down!

18:41.370 --> 18:44.289
Our heads? But he can't see!

19:05.227 --> 19:07.062
Tsukasa and Hyoga got hit!

19:09.148 --> 19:12.317
I'm fine... My life is not in danger...

19:15.028 --> 19:17.321
<i>If he shoots at the motorbikes
inside the smoke screen,</i>

19:17.322 --> 19:19.407
<i>Xeno could get caught
in the crossfire and die.</i>

19:19.408 --> 19:22.953
<i>Then the best move is
to take out our main force.</i>

19:23.912 --> 19:26.539
<i>He decided to eliminate our aces,</i>

19:26.540 --> 19:28.208
<i>Tsukasa and Hyoga...</i>

19:35.007 --> 19:39.136
Xeno will definitely
leave some clues for us.

19:40.095 --> 19:44.516
No matter the situation, that's how he is.

19:46.059 --> 19:50.022
That's Morse Code.
I have a brief memory of it!

20:01.909 --> 20:04.536
I hear an engine!
They're about to take off on their plane!

20:05.704 --> 20:07.997
So the combat aircraft's operational range

20:07.998 --> 20:10.499
is the third and largest net, huh?

20:10.500 --> 20:14.295
We won't be able to run away from that!

20:14.296 --> 20:15.380
Yes...

20:16.506 --> 20:19.342
If a combat aircraft
flies over us in the desert,

20:19.343 --> 20:21.470
we won't stand a chance of surviving.

20:23.013 --> 20:25.224
Only if it were to fly over us, though...

20:30.562 --> 20:31.647
It's coming!

20:35.275 --> 20:36.943
- What?
- I don't know!

20:36.944 --> 20:39.571
The engine suddenly broke down!

20:46.662 --> 20:50.164
The science bomb finally exploded!

20:50.165 --> 20:53.793
Yes, after the dogfight crash,

20:53.794 --> 20:56.505
when we had to
ditch our plane and split up...

20:57.297 --> 21:00.883
<i>No fool would honestly hand over a weapon</i>

21:00.884 --> 21:04.470
<i>that's ten billion percent sure
to be taken by the enemy.</i>

21:04.471 --> 21:08.766
<i>I made sure to pour that souvenir
sugar water straight into their tank.</i>

21:08.767 --> 21:09.685
SUGAR WATER

21:10.894 --> 21:14.189
I've heard about that before!
If you mix sugar with gasoline...

21:16.108 --> 21:20.987
It still runs for a moment,
so they won't notice during inspection.

21:20.988 --> 21:25.075
By the time they realize it,
the sugar's turned into carbon sludge,

21:25.909 --> 21:29.204
and with those primitive engines,
it seizes up and it's already too late.

21:53.478 --> 21:57.607
All right!
We broke through Stanley's blockade!

22:00.444 --> 22:04.323
On our motorbikes,
all the way across South America!

22:08.493 --> 22:11.705
Yes, we're getting closer.

22:12.205 --> 22:14.583
To the source of the petrification!

23:53.348 --> 23:55.933
NEXT EPISODE

23:55.934 --> 23:57.935
THIS WORK OF FICTION FEATURES ORGANISMS,
MATERIAL AND PROCESSES BASED ON REAL LIFE.

23:57.936 --> 23:59.728
COLLECTING OR REPLICATING THEM
MAY BE DANGEROUS AND ILLEGAL,

23:59.729 --> 24:00.938
SO DO NOT ATTEMPT.

24:00.939 --> 24:01.940
Subtitle translation by: Silvia
